Collection Processing
Collection processing is working with a Collection using function using Function instead of Collections. This way we don't have to write multiple Loops ands If Staments.
Kotlin uses Processing Flow to make sure one function does one job.
Collection Function used in Kotlin:

forEach - Makes a change or performs an operation for each Item.
Syntax:
collection.forEach { element ->
    // action
}

onEach - Makes a change or performs an operation for each Item and keeps the List.
Syntax:
collection.onEach {element->
    //action
}

filter - Removes item that doesn't match the conditions specified.
Syntax:
collection.filter { condition }

map - Operation is performed on all the elements of a collection.
Syntax:
collection.filter{ operation }

flatmap - map but combines the result. Meaning if collection.flatmap(it,it+2) then the collection will be changed from [1,2] to [1,3,2,4]
Eg:
val lists = listOf(listOf(1, 2), listOf(3, 4))
val result = lists.flatMap { it } // [1, 2, 3, 4]

fold - Combine all the elements in the given collection. Prefered to be used when there is a Zero Value.
val numbers = listOf(1, 2, 3)
val result = numbers.fold(10) { initial, element -> initial + element } // 16 (10 + 1 + 2 + 3)

reduce - Combine items into one result using the first item. It is faster than fold.
Eg:
val numbers = listOf(1, 2, 3)
val result = numbers.reduce { accumulator, element -> accumulator + element } // 6 (1 + 2 + 3)

sum - add all the elements and returns result
Syntax:
collection.sum()

count - counts the item in the Collection
Syntax:
collection.count()

any - Returns if at least one item true.
Eg:
val numbers = listOf(1, 2, 3)
val result = numbers.any { it > 2 } // true

all - Return if all the items true.
Eg:
val numbers = listOf(1, 2, 3)
val result = numbers.all { it > 0 } // true

none - Returns if no items true.
Eg:
val numbers = listOf(1, 2, 3)
val result = numbers.none { it > 5 } // true

groupby - Groups items based on one some condition
Eg:
val words = listOf("apple", "banana", "apricot")
val result = words.groupBy { it.first() } // {a=[apple, apricot], b=[banana]}

distinct - Remove duplicates
Syntax:
collection.distinct()

sorted - Sort items in ascending order
Syntax:
collection.sorted()

take - Take first items
Eg:
val numbers = listOf(1, 2, 3, 4)
val result = numbers.take(2) // [1, 2]

drop - Skip first items
Eg:
val numbers = listOf(1, 2, 3, 4)
val result = numbers.drop(2) // [3, 4]

joinToString - Convert collection to a string
Eg:
val letters = listOf("A", "B", "C")
val result = letters.joinToString(separator = "-", prefix = "[", postfix = "]") // "[A-B-C]"

runningFold - like fold but shows intermediate Values
Syntax:
numbers.runningFold(0) { acc, i -> acc + i }

scan - does the samething as runnning fold
Syntax:
numbers.scan(0) { acc, i -> acc + i }

hasNext - checks if there is a next Element in the List
Syntax:
collection.hasNext()

sumOf - It extracts a countable value for each element and then sums these values.
Eg:
println(players.sumOf { it.points })

withIndex - Gives Index to every element in the Collection.
Syntax:
collection.withIndex()

filterIndexed - same as withIndexed but gives after filtering. This works on each element of the Collection.
Eg:
collection.filterIndexed { index, value -> index % 2 == 0 }

mapIndexed - does what withIndex does but on particular elements of the Collection.
Eg:
collection..mapIndexed { index, value -> "[$index] $value" }

takeLast(n) - returns a collection with only the last n elements (or returns the unchanged collection if it has less than n elements).
Eg:
val numbers = listOf(1, 2, 3, 4)
val result = numbers.takeLast(2) // [3, 4]

dropLast(n) - returns a collection without the last n elements.
Eg:
val numbers = listOf(1, 2, 3, 4)
val result = numbers.dropLast(2) // [1, 2]

subList - gives SubCollection of a Collection between the index.
Eg:
print(collection.subList(start,end))

get - get element from the specified position or indexed.
Syntax:
collection.get()

find - find elements that fulfill given condition.Just an alias for firstOrNull
Syntax:
collection.find{Condition}

partition - Divides the Collection according to given condition.
Syntax:
collection.partition{condition}

distinctBy - it is just distinct with a selector.
Syntax:
collection.distinctBy{condition/selector}

sortedBy - Sorted but with condition. Also has a Descending function.
Syntax:
collection.sortedBy{condition}

reversed - reverses Collection.
Syntax:
collection.reversed()

sortDescending- Sorts in Descending order.
syntax:
collection.sortDescending()

shuffled - make the order of the Collection random. has Or Null Variant.
Syntax:
collection.shuffled()

random - gives random element from the list. Has a OrNull Variant..
Syntax:
collection.random()

zip - connect two collection. It only connects equal number of element and discard any extra.
Syntax:
println(nums.zip(chars))
// [(1, A), (2, B), (3, C), (4, D)]

.zipWithNext - Zip but instead of using two collection it uses the same collection. n and n+1 is taken then it moves to n+2 and n+3.
Syntax:
println(person.zipWithNext { prev, next -> "$prev$next" })